# XBD 9.1 Regular Expression Definitions
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Tested through regcomp()/regexec() in the POSIX locale (the tests never
# call setlocale()), plus one utility test of the "invalid" rule. Matching
# order and sub-match rules that XSH.regcomp already tests are referenced
# from the .cov, not repeated here.

assertion  XBD.9.1-definitions.001
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.1
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.3.2
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.4.2
class      required
since      1992
says       Every character of the POSIX locale other than the NUL and the special
           characters is an RE that matches exactly itself, in a BRE and in an ERE:
           searching a string holding bytes 1 to 255 in order, and again in reverse
           order, finds the single-byte pattern at that byte's own position, so no
           other byte is taken for it.
test       t/001-ordinary-bytes.c

assertion  XBD.9.1-definitions.002
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.1
class      required
since      1992
says       The match begins at the earliest position where any match exists, even if
           a longer match begins later, and among matches from that position the
           longest is taken whatever the order of alternatives: a|bcd and bcd|a on
           abcd give the first character, d|bcd on abcd gives characters two to four,
           x* on abxxx is empty at offset 0.
test       t/002-earliest-longest.c

assertion  XBD.9.1-definitions.003
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.1
class      required
since      1992
says       The whole match is the longest first; the subpatterns then take the
           longest strings left to right that are consistent with it: (a*)(b|abc) on
           abc gives group one empty and group two abc, (ab|a)(bc|c)? on abc gives ab
           and c, (a*)(ab)*(b*) on aabb gives aa, no participation, bb, and \(.*\)\(.*\)
           puts everything in group one.
test       t/003-subpattern-order.c

assertion  XBD.9.1-definitions.004
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.1
class      required
since      1992
says       A backslash that is itself escaped does not begin another escape sequence:
           in a BRE \(a\)\\1 matches a, a backslash and the digit 1 (not a back-
           reference), \\* repeats the literal backslash, and in an ERE \\. is a
           backslash followed by any character, \\(a) a backslash and a group, \\+
           a run of backslashes and \\|b either.
test       t/004-escape-sequence.c

assertion  XBD.9.1-definitions.005
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.1
class      required
since      1992
says       A standard utility given an invalid RE (a back-reference to a group that
           does not exist, a collating symbol naming no collating element) generates
           an error condition: grep (BRE and ERE) exits with a value above 1 and sed
           with a nonzero value.
test       t/005-invalid-utility.sh
